  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nA low stratus deck is in place over the forecast area early this \nmorning. Northeasterly flow is also present, creating upslope flow \nalong the Prairie Coteau. Between the two, it has not been difficult \nfor fog to form over that area, and as a result a Dense Fog Advisory \nhas been issued for parts of northeastern South Dakota. Further \nexpansions of the Advisory will be possible depending on how other \nareas evolve this morning. Due to the persistence of cloud cover \nafter sunrise today, fog may take a while to burn off, so currently \nthe Advisory is in place through 18Z. \n\nRain showers are also moving into north central South Dakota this \nmorning, although mainly just some light drizzle as of ~0630Z. \nShowers will progress slowly east through the day today, mainly over \nnorth central and northeastern South Dakota as well as western \nMinnesota. Rain rates will remain fairly light, and only a few \nhundredths to perhaps a tenth of an inch at most is expected. Some \nadditional chances for rain showers will be present over central and \nnorth central South Dakota Tuesday as well, but once again not \nexpecting any substantial rainfall, just a couple hundredths at the \nmost. \n\nChances for rain return once again Wednesday evening into Thursday. \nAn upper-level low sitting over the western CONUS will move slowly \nnorth, but model trends show a more progressive evolution to the \neast (keeping the upper-level low to the south) rather than previous \nruns where the low moved further north. As a result, ensemble \nguidance continues to back off on the totals Wednesday into \nThursday. Expectations now are just a few hundredths of accumulation \nin total. Latest NBM probabilities of seeing a tenth of an inch or \nmore through the event sit at 20-50%, increasing as you move from \nnorth to south across the forecast area. These numbers are down from \nthe 40-60% that was in place 24 hours ago. \n\nTemperatures through the work week are expected to be near-normal. \nAs a result, overnight lows will drop into the mid-30s to perhaps \neven freezing over the next few days. This will mainly occur over \nthe northern parts of the forecast area, but widespread frost will \nbe possible, particularly on Wednesday and Friday mornings. Chances \nfor a hard freeze (28 degrees or lower) are fairly low through the \nweek.",
